# Builds Kerberos 5 **with the OpenSSL TLS module**, for one test and
# nothing else.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IMAGE IS NOT THE ORACLE. It is a client-side test fixture.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BOOTSTRAP.md §3.3 says this project does not link OpenSSL, and that
# rule is **product-scoped**: it applies to everything the project
# ships and to every other test. Here is why the narrowing is honest
# rather than convenient.
#
# The rule's own justification was that "upstream's k5tls plugin is
# the only part of Kerberos 5 that wants it" -- which is an argument
# about what a *KDC* needs, and this project's KDC needs none of it.
# k5tls is a **client** plugin: it is what lets an unmodified
# Kerberos 5 client reach an `https://' KDC with no shim in front of
# it. Applying a rule about the product to a fixture that tests
# whether other people's clients can reach the product was a stricter
# reading than the argument supported, and it had a cost: §6 had to
# admit that the TLS leg was exercised by kdiamond-proxy and not by
# any client.
#
# So: the ordinary oracle (Containerfile.krb5) stays OpenSSL-free and
# every differential case keeps running against it. This image exists
# for the single case that proves a stock client negotiates the TLS
# itself, and the image name says so.
#
# A diagnostic trap worth knowing, and the reason this image is
# necessary rather than merely nice: with --with-tls-impl=no the
# module still **loads successfully**. notls.c:40-51 compiles the
# init symbol and leaves the vtable nulled, so the load-error trace
# line never fires; setup == NULL then makes setup_tls return false
# and service_https_write kills the connection
# (sendto_kdc.c:1266-1269) **with no trace output at all**. A client
# from the ordinary oracle pointed at an https:// KDC does not report
# a missing TLS module -- it reports "cannot contact any KDC". Nobody
# would diagnose that from the outside.

FROM docker.io/library/debian:12-slim AS build

# libssl-dev is the difference from the ordinary oracle. Everything
# else in this stage is identical, deliberately: the only variable
# under test is the TLS module.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        build-essential autoconf m4 bison perl python3 \
        ca-certificates libssl-dev \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

WORKDIR /src
COPY . .

WORKDIR /src/src

RUN autoconf -f --include=. && autoheader --include=.

# --with-tls-impl=openssl, and that is the whole point of this file.
#
# configure.ac:290-331 accepts literally `openssl', `auto' or `no'
# and errors on anything else (:320-322); no other backend has ever
# existed upstream, and k5-tls.h says of its own interface that it is
# "currently tailored for the needs of the OpenSSL module ... can be
# changed without regard for backward compatibility" (:33-39). So
# this is not one choice among several -- it is the only way to get a
# Kerberos 5 client that speaks HTTPS.
#
# Only the *backend* is gated by the flag. The shared object is built
# either way, which is why --with-tls-impl=no still produces a
# loadable module and why the trap described above exists.
#
# PKINIT stays disabled. OpenSSL is present now and configure would
# happily build it, but PKINIT is a declared non-goal and building it
# here would mean this fixture exercised a code path the project has
# no intention of implementing.
RUN ./configure \
        --prefix=/opt/krb5 \
        --with-tls-impl=openssl \
        --disable-pkinit \
        --disable-nls \
        --disable-rpath \
    && make -j"$(nproc)" \
    && make install

FROM docker.io/library/debian:12-slim

# Unlike the ordinary oracle, this stage *does* install a package:
# libssl3, which k5tls.so is linked against and cannot run without.
# It is the one line that separates the two images at runtime.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        libssl3 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

COPY --from=build /opt/krb5 /opt/krb5
ENV PATH=/opt/krb5/sbin:/opt/krb5/bin:$PATH \
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/krb5/lib

# No realm-setup and no entrypoint of its own. This image is a
# *client*: it never serves a realm, and the one test that uses it
# writes the client configuration in and runs kinit. Giving it a KDC
# to run would invite somebody to compare against it, which is
# exactly what it must not be used for.
ENV TZ=UTC \
    LC_ALL=C
ENTRYPOINT ["sleep", "infinity"]
